WEBVTT

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:05.758
Optimisers.

00:00:06.078 --> 00:00:07.383
Better ways to step.

00:00:07.983 --> 00:00:11.330
Last time I walked downhill one careful step at a time.

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.745
Tonight the walker gets weight, and then a pair of shoes.

00:00:15.945 --> 00:00:18.303
Remember the long, narrow valley.

00:00:18.623 --> 00:00:20.623
Steep across, nearly flat along.

00:00:21.223 --> 00:00:25.834
Careful steps have to stay small for the steep way, so along the floor they crawl.

00:00:26.154 --> 00:00:33.101
At the best step we tried, it takes 6,217 steps to get the loss under a ten-thousandth and keep it there.

00:00:34.301 --> 00:00:35.964
Now make the walker heavy.

00:00:36.284 --> 00:00:41.063
Each step keeps 90 percent of the last step's motion and adds the new slope on top.

00:00:41.663 --> 00:00:44.989
Across the valley, the slopes keep flipping, so they cancel.

00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:48.530
Along the floor they all point the same way, so they add up.

00:00:48.850 --> 00:00:51.819
The walker picks up speed exactly where it was crawling.

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:55.282
Boris Polyak described it in 1964.

00:00:55.602 --> 00:00:57.433
It's known as the heavy ball method.

00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:01.307
The other idea is shoes that adjust to the ground.

00:01:01.627 --> 00:01:05.985
Adam keeps a running average of each slope, and a running average of each slope squared.

00:01:06.585 --> 00:01:09.153
It divides the first by the square root of the second.

00:01:09.473 --> 00:01:18.610
So a direction with a steady slope gets a step of about the same size, however steep or gentle it is: steep directions are slowed down, gentle ones sped up.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.857
A direction whose slope keeps flipping gets a short step.

00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:26.035
Both averages start at zero, so at first they're too small.

00:01:26.355 --> 00:01:35.113
Adam scales them back up, so its very first step is the step size you chose, to within a hair, in every direction that has any slope at all.

00:01:36.313 --> 00:01:38.124
Same valley, same start.

00:01:38.444 --> 00:01:41.496
Which walker gets down this valley first?

00:01:43.996 --> 00:01:46.817
Each walker gets the best step from a few we tried.

00:01:47.417 --> 00:01:50.828
The heavy ball, by a mile: 285 steps.

00:01:51.148 --> 00:01:54.558
Careful steps take 6,217.

00:01:54.878 --> 00:02:03.931
Adam dips under the line at step 10,346, keeps wandering around it, and only stays under after 16,790.

00:02:04.531 --> 00:02:12.847
And on the gentle bowl from last time, careful steps win: 33 steps, against 146 for the other two.

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:15.756
No walker is best on every hill.

00:02:16.956 --> 00:02:21.798
Remember the edge on the bowl: past a step of 0.5, careful steps fly off.

00:02:22.118 --> 00:02:23.739
Try 0.6.

00:02:24.339 --> 00:02:27.224
Careful steps fly off, as before.

00:02:27.544 --> 00:02:31.817
The heavy ball's edge here is 0.95, so it gets down.

00:02:32.137 --> 00:02:38.095
Adam's steps don't grow with the slope, so it doesn't fly off, and here it gets down too.

00:02:39.295 --> 00:02:41.443
The step size doesn't have to stay fixed.

00:02:41.763 --> 00:02:49.320
Many models start with a warmup, small steps while everything is new, then let the step size fall away along a smooth curve.

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:59.478
And AdamW adds one more thing: almost every number shrinks by a small fraction at each step, kept separate from Adam's own step.

00:02:59.798 --> 00:03:03.251
It pulls numbers that aren't earning their keep back towards zero.

00:03:04.451 --> 00:03:17.798
On the spirals from the bucket chain, with each walker's step chosen on a separate set of points, careful steps first pass 95 percent on new points at step 2,000, the heavy ball at 1,300, and Adam at 900.

00:03:18.398 --> 00:03:30.167
But run five different starting networks and batch orders, and Adam's final score ranges from 91.8 to 99.3 percent, while careful steps stay between 99.0 and 99.8.

00:03:30.487 --> 00:03:33.855
Fast, and on this network, less steady.

00:03:34.455 --> 00:03:37.845
The language model from the attention lesson trains with AdamW.

00:03:38.165 --> 00:03:50.754
For its first 400 steps, with the same start and the same sentences, AdamW reaches a held-out loss of 0.85, the heavy ball 0.89, and careful steps 0.96.

00:03:51.954 --> 00:03:56.438
Every number tonight depends on the hill and on the handful of steps we tried for each walker.

00:03:56.758 --> 00:04:00.274
Try more, or a different hill, and the order can change.

00:04:00.874 --> 00:04:04.790
And four hundred steps is the first fifth of the language model's training.

00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:11.510
It has barely started to copy, so this shows who gets off the line fastest, not who finishes best.

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.731
Weight carries you through the zigzags.

00:04:15.051 --> 00:04:17.556
Shoes give every direction its own stride.

00:04:17.876 --> 00:04:19.560
Neither wins every hill.

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.097
Next along the hill: learning a harbour by heart, and why that's a trap.

00:04:24.417 --> 00:04:25.070
Goodnight.

